    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms7869",
    "abstract": "Abstract Tropical Pacific sea surface temperature anomalies influence the atmospheric circulation, impacting climate far beyond the tropics. The predictability of the corresponding atmospheric signals is typically limited to less than 1 year lead time. Here we present observational and modelling evidence for multi-year predictability of coherent trans-basin climate variations that are characterized by a zonal seesaw in tropical sea surface temperature and sea-level pressure between the Pacific and the other two ocean basins. State-of-the-art climate model forecasts initialized from a realistic ocean state show that the low-frequency trans-basin climate variability, which explains part of the El Ni\u00f1o Southern Oscillation flavours, can be predicted up to 3 years ahead, thus exceeding the predictive skill of current tropical climate forecasts for natural variability. This low-frequency variability emerges from the synchronization of ocean anomalies in all basins via global reorganizations of the atmospheric Walker Circulation.",
    "field": "Decadal climate prediction",
    "problem": "The predictability of tropical climate variations is typically limited to less than one year, preventing skillful multi-year forecasts of natural atmospheric and oceanic signals that influence global climate.",
    "method": "The authors perform multi-year hindcasts using a coupled climate model initialized from a realistic ocean state, which is generated by assimilating observed oceanic temperature and salinity data, to assess the predictability of tropical trans-basin climate variability and identify its sources.",
    "task": "The task is to demonstrate and explain the multi-year predictability of coherent climate variations across tropical ocean basins using initialized climate model forecasts, showing predictive skill up to three years ahead.",
    "model": [
        "MIROC"
    ],
